<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Balwant Singh of Benares</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 1740–1770</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Balwant Singh of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Benares</a> was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa">Mansa</a> Ram, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhumihar">Bhumihar</a> landholder who obtained the zamindari of Benares under the Nawab of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a>. He turned the family's holdings into a semi-independent principality and built Ramnagar Fort on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> opposite Varanasi. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chait-singh">Chait Singh</a> later came into conflict with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warren-hastings">Warren Hastings</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/balwant-singh-of-benares.jpg" alt="Balwant Singh of Benares" width="320" height="473" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sunder Sharma · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maharaja_Balwant_Singh_of_Benares.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
